1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Here is the problem: changing one warehouse column
can break systems your repository cannot even see.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
In our test, repository search found one consumer.
DataHub found thirty-one, across seven pages.
It changes whether the column can be removed.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Retirement Conductor moves the consumers we can change,
runs their real tests, checks the graph again,
and then drops the old field—or stops.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Here is the actual agent run. I ask Codex to replace legacy_status
with order_status. It uses DataHub and our MCP server,
finds the exact dbt model, and produces a one-file plan.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:55,000
And then it stops. The agent cannot approve its own change.
I authorize that exact plan in a separate operator terminal.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:18,000
That opens a real pull request.
dbt parse, build, test, and semantic checks all pass.
The result is written back to DataHub, read back,
and tied to the same campaign.

7
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Now for the part I cared about: does it actually do anything?
A separately privileged command checks everything again
and really drops the PostgreSQL column. One drop.
The replacement survives, Spark stays healthy, and replay is refused.

8
00:01:43,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Then we make it harder. After approval, a new Spark consumer
appears in DataHub, still reading legacy_status.
The answer flips to unsafe.

9
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The same final command now commits nothing, and the old column stays.
This is not a staged red badge: in the static-signoff control,
the drop goes through and Spark actually fails with SQLSTATE 42703.

10
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The Workbench is the readable view of that same campaign:
what changed, what passed, what is fresh, and why we stopped.
We do not pretend DataHub sees the universe.
We make the evidence explicit—and when it changes,
the database change does not happen.
